# CASP15 T1158v3 — The Steroid Shuttle (8SX7)
---

MRP4 doesn't just pump prostaglandins.

Here it is bound to DHEAS — dehydroepiandrosterone
sulfate — a steroid hormone precursor.

This shows that MRP4 is a promiscuous transporter
with multiple roles in the body.

> close
> load 8sx7
---

DHEAS is the most abundant steroid hormone
in the human body. It's a precursor to both
testosterone and estrogen.

The fact that MRP4 transports it means this
pump plays a role in hormone signaling
we're only beginning to understand.

> spin 0.15
---

Look at how differently DHEAS sits in the
binding pocket compared to the prostaglandins.

It's a completely different shape — bulky steroid
rings versus the long fatty acid chain of
prostaglandins — yet MRP4 accommodates both.

> stop
> view ligand
---

This kind of substrate promiscuity is a nightmare
for docking prediction. How do you predict
where a molecule binds when the pocket
can reshape to fit wildly different shapes?

That's what makes CASP15 such a valuable
benchmark.

> reset
> spin 0.1
---

MRP4 with DHEAS — one pump, many cargoes.
PDB code 8SX7, CASP15 target T1158v3.

> stop
